Task 1: Configure Kerberos for Single Sign-On (SSO)
Subtask 1.1: Set Up the Kerberos KDC Server
Step 1: Connect to the KDC Server

Access your KDC server machine and switch to root user:

sudo su -
Step 2: Install Kerberos Server Packages

Install the required Kerberos server packages:

yum install -y krb5-server krb5-libs krb5-workstation
Step 3: Configure the Kerberos Database

Edit the KDC configuration file:

vi /etc/krb5kdc/kdc.conf
Replace the contents with:

[kdcdefaults]
 kdc_ports = 88
 kdc_tcp_ports = 88

[realms]
 EXAMPLE.COM = {
  #master_key_type = aes256-cts
  acl_file = /etc/krb5kdc/kadm5.acl
  dict_file = /usr/share/dict/words
  admin_keytab = /etc/krb5kdc/kadm5.keytab
  supported_enctypes = aes256-cts:normal aes128-cts:normal des3-hmac-sha1:normal arcfour-hmac:normal camellia256-cts:normal camellia128-cts:normal des-hmac-sha1:normal des-cbc-md5:normal des-cbc-crc:normal
 }
Step 4: Configure Kerberos Client Settings

Edit the main Kerberos configuration file:

vi /etc/krb5.conf
Replace the contents with:

[logging]
 default = FILE:/var/log/krb5libs.log
 kdc = FILE:/var/log/krb5kdc.log
 admin_server = FILE:/var/log/kadmind.log

[libdefaults]
 dns_lookup_realm = false
 ticket_lifetime = 24h
 renew_lifetime = 7d
 forwardable = true
 rdns = false
 pkinit_anchors = FILE:/etc/pki/tls/certs/ca-bundle.crt
 default_realm = EXAMPLE.COM
 default_ccache_name = KEYRING:persistent:%{uid}

[realms]
 EXAMPLE.COM = {
  kdc = kdc-server.example.com
  admin_server = kdc-server.example.com
 }

[domain_realm]
 .example.com = EXAMPLE.COM
 example.com = EXAMPLE.COM
Step 5: Create the Kerberos Database

Initialize the Kerberos database:

kdb5_util create -s -r EXAMPLE.COM
When prompted, enter a master password (remember this password).

Step 6: Configure Administrative Access

Edit the ACL file to define administrative permissions:

vi /etc/krb5kdc/kadm5.acl
Add the following line:

*/admin@EXAMPLE.COM    *
Step 7: Start and Enable Kerberos Services

Start the KDC and admin services:

systemctl start krb5kdc
systemctl start kadmin
systemctl enable krb5kdc
systemctl enable kadmin
Step 8: Configure Firewall

Open the necessary ports:

firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=88/tcp
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=88/udp
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=749/tcp
firewall-cmd --reload
Subtask 1.2: Create Kerberos Principals
Step 1: Access Kerberos Administration

Connect to the Kerberos administrative interface:

kadmin.local
Step 2: Create Administrative Principal

Create an admin principal:

addprinc admin/admin@EXAMPLE.COM
Enter a password when prompted.

Step 3: Create User Principals

Create test user principals:

addprinc testuser1@EXAMPLE.COM
addprinc testuser2@EXAMPLE.COM
addprinc ssouser@EXAMPLE.COM
Set passwords for each user when prompted.

Step 4: Create Host Principals

Create host principals for client machines:

addprinc -randkey host/client1.example.com@EXAMPLE.COM
addprinc -randkey host/client2.example.com@EXAMPLE.COM
Step 5: Exit Kerberos Administration

quit
Subtask 1.3: Configure Kerberos Clients
Step 1: Connect to Client1

Switch to the client1 machine and become root:

sudo su -
Step 2: Install Kerberos Client Packages

yum install -y krb5-workstation krb5-libs
Step 3: Configure Client Kerberos Settings

Copy the krb5.conf from the KDC server or create it manually:

vi /etc/krb5.conf
Use the same configuration as created on the KDC server in Subtask 1.1, Step 4.

Step 4: Configure Host Resolution

Ensure proper hostname resolution by editing the hosts file:

vi /etc/hosts
Add entries for your lab environment:

192.168.1.10    kdc-server.example.com kdc-server
192.168.1.11    client1.example.com client1
192.168.1.12    client2.example.com client2
Note: Replace IP addresses with your actual lab machine IPs.

Step 5: Repeat Client Configuration

Repeat steps 1-4 for client2 machine.

Task 2: Test SSO with kinit and klist
Subtask 2.1: Basic Authentication Testing
Step 1: Test Initial Authentication

On client1, test authentication with a user principal:

kinit testuser1@EXAMPLE.COM
Enter the password when prompted.

Step 2: Verify Ticket Cache

Check the current Kerberos tickets:

klist
You should see output similar to:

Ticket cache: KEYRING:persistent:1000:1000
Default principal: testuser1@EXAMPLE.COM

Valid starting       Expires              Service principal
12/01/2023 10:00:00  12/02/2023 10:00:00  krbtgt/EXAMPLE.COM@EXAMPLE.COM
        renew until 12/08/2023 10:00:00
Step 3: Test Ticket Information

Get detailed ticket information:

klist -f
This shows additional flags and encryption types.

Subtask 2.2: Advanced Ticket Management
Step 1: Test Ticket Renewal

Renew the current ticket:

kinit -R
Step 2: Test Different Users

Switch to a different user:

kinit ssouser@EXAMPLE.COM
klist
Step 3: Test Ticket Destruction

Destroy current tickets:

kdestroy
klist
You should see a message indicating no credentials cache found.

Step 4: Test Forwardable Tickets

Create forwardable tickets:

kinit -f testuser1@EXAMPLE.COM
klist -f
Look for the F flag indicating forwardable tickets.

Subtask 2.3: Cross-Client Testing
Step 1: Test from Client2

Switch to client2 and test authentication:

kinit testuser2@EXAMPLE.COM
klist
Step 2: Test Network Connectivity

Verify KDC connectivity:

telnet kdc-server.example.com 88
Press Ctrl+C to exit if connection is successful.

Task 3: Automate Kerberos SSO Login on Client Systems
Subtask 3.1: Configure PAM for Kerberos Authentication
Step 1: Install Additional Packages

On both client machines, install PAM Kerberos module:

yum install -y pam_krb5
Step 2: Configure PAM Authentication

Edit the system authentication configuration:

vi /etc/pam.d/system-auth
Add the following lines after the existing auth lines:

auth        sufficient    pam_krb5.so use_first_pass
account     [default=bad success=ok user_unknown=ignore] pam_krb5.so
password    sufficient    pam_krb5.so use_authtok
session     optional      pam_krb5.so
Step 3: Configure Password Authentication

Edit the password authentication file:

vi /etc/pam.d/password-auth
Add the same lines as in Step 2.

Subtask 3.2: Create Automatic Login Scripts
Step 1: Create Kerberos Login Script

Create a script for automatic Kerberos authentication:

vi /usr/local/bin/kerberos-login.sh
Add the following content:

#!/bin/bash

# Kerberos SSO Login Script
# Usage: kerberos-login.sh username

USERNAME=$1
REALM="EXAMPLE.COM"

if [ -z "$USERNAME" ]; then
    echo "Usage: $0 <username>"
    exit 1
fi

# Check if user already has valid tickets
if klist -s 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "Valid Kerberos tickets already exist for $(klist | grep 'Default principal' | cut -d: -f2 | tr -d ' ')"
    exit 0
fi

# Prompt for password and authenticate
echo "Authenticating $USERNAME@$REALM..."
kinit $USERNAME@$REALM

if [ $? -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "Authentication successful!"
    echo "Current tickets:"
    klist
else
    echo "Authentication failed!"
    exit 1
fi
Step 2: Make Script Executable

chmod +x /usr/local/bin/kerberos-login.sh
Step 3: Test the Login Script

/usr/local/bin/kerberos-login.sh testuser1
Subtask 3.3: Configure Automatic Ticket Renewal
Step 1: Create Ticket Renewal Script

vi /usr/local/bin/kerberos-renew.sh
Add the following content:

#!/bin/bash

# Kerberos Ticket Renewal Script

# Check if tickets exist and are renewable
if klist -s 2>/dev/null; then
    # Try to renew tickets
    kinit -R 2>/dev/null
    
    if [ $? -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "$(date): Kerberos tickets renewed successfully" >> /var/log/kerberos-renew.log
    else
        echo "$(date): Failed to renew Kerberos tickets" >> /var/log/kerberos-renew.log
    fi
else
    echo "$(date): No valid Kerberos tickets found" >> /var/log/kerberos-renew.log
fi
Step 2: Make Renewal Script Executable

chmod +x /usr/local/bin/kerberos-renew.sh
Step 3: Configure Cron Job for Automatic Renewal

Add a cron job to automatically renew tickets:

crontab -e
Add the following line to run every hour:

0 * * * * /usr/local/bin/kerberos-renew.sh
Subtask 3.4: Configure Desktop Environment Integration
Step 1: Create Desktop Login Script

For systems with desktop environments, create a login script:

vi /etc/profile.d/kerberos-sso.sh
Add the following content:

#!/bin/bash

# Kerberos SSO Profile Script

# Function to check and obtain Kerberos tickets
kerberos_sso_check() {
    if ! klist -s 2>/dev/null; then
        echo "No valid Kerberos tickets found."
        echo "Please run: kinit <username>@EXAMPLE.COM"
    else
        echo "Valid Kerberos tickets found:"
        klist | grep "Default principal"
    fi
}

# Create alias for easy ticket checking
alias kcheck='kerberos_sso_check'
alias klogin='/usr/local/bin/kerberos-login.sh'
Step 2: Make Profile Script Executable

chmod +x /etc/profile.d/kerberos-sso.sh
Step 3: Test Profile Integration

Log out and log back in, then test:

kcheck
